  "number": "1.5",
  "title": "Venn Diagrams",
  "body": " Venn Diagrams       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.1 Sets    Specific Learning Outcomes  Draw Venn diagrams to represent sets           Venn Diagrams with Two Sets   Multiples of Two and Three   , is the set of multiples of in and is the set of multiples of in .   A blank Venn diagram for the sets and .   A rectangle for the universal set with two overlapping circles labelled T and H. No numbers are written in yet.        Copy the diagram and write each number from to in the region where it belongs.    Find , , and .    Find a rule that links the four numbers in part (b).        Two-Set Venn Diagrams  In a Venn diagram the universal set is a rectangle and each set is a circle, labelled outside the circle. Elements, or counts of elements, are written in the regions. In this book the four regions of a two-set diagram are numbered as in : region is in only, region is in both, region is in only and region is outside both. So is regions , is and is .  For counting, fill the region that belongs to the most sets first, then work outwards. Adding and counts region twice, so    The four regions of a two-set Venn diagram.   A rectangle for the universal set with two overlapping circles A and B. Region 1 is A only, region 2 is both, region 3 is B only, region 4 is outside both.      Complements by Region   Give the region numbers of , , and .    and . , so . . These are two ways to describe the outside of both circles.     Agriculture and Computer Studies   In a class of learners, take Agriculture, take Computer Studies and take both. How many take Agriculture only, Computer Studies only, and neither? Find by the formula.    Fill the overlap first: . Agriculture only ; Computer Studies only ; neither . By the formula, .   Agriculture and Computer Studies in a class of .   A two-set Venn diagram, Agriculture and Computer Studies. Agriculture only 19, both 9, Computer Studies only 13, neither 4.  Region counts: Agriculture only ; both ; Computer Studies only ; neither . Total .        Finding the Overlap   In a youth group of , have a smartphone, have a bicycle and have neither. How many have both?    . Then .    Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example {2, 5, 9} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Type the empty set as {} . Type a count as a whole number.        , is the set of multiples of and is the set of odd numbers in . Draw the Venn diagram. List and the elements outside both circles.  only ; ; only ; outside both .    Using the region numbers of , give the regions of (a) (b) (c) .  (a) (b) (c)    Of learners, play a sport, sing in the choir and do neither. How many do both? How many play a sport only? How many sing in the choir only?  Both ; sport only ; choir only  , so both . Sport only and choir only .    , and . Find .     , and . Find .     Challenge: Must They Meet?  , and . Can and have no element in common?  No. , so at least elements are in both.       Venn Diagrams with Three Sets   Three Subjects   Twenty learners said which of Mathematics ( ), Kiswahili ( ) and Agriculture ( ) they like.   Subjects Liked by Twenty Learners   Learner Mathematics Kiswahili Agriculture  Akinyi yes yes  Brian yes  Chepkoech yes  David yes  Esther yes yes yes  Fatuma yes yes  George yes  Halima yes  Ibrahim yes  Jeptoo yes yes  Kamau yes yes yes  Lilian yes  Mwangi yes yes  Nafula yes yes  Otieno yes  Pendo yes  Rehema  Samson  Tabitha  Wekesa yes       Draw a three-set Venn diagram and write the number of learners in each region.    Find , and .    How many learners like exactly two of the subjects? How many like at least one?    Enter your own class's counts in and check your totals.        Three-Set Venn Diagrams  A three-set diagram has eight regions, numbered as in : in only, in and only, in only, in and only, in all three, in and only, in only, outside all three.  When a survey gives the set totals, the pair totals and the number in all three, fill the region that belongs to the most sets first, then work outwards:  Write the number in all three in the centre.  Each exactly two region is its pair total minus the centre.  Each only region is its set total minus its two exactly two regions minus the centre.  The number in none is the total minus the seven inner regions.  Check every set total.      The eight regions of a three-set Venn diagram.   Three overlapping circles A, B and C in a rectangle, with the eight regions numbered 1 to 8: 1 A only, 2 A and B only, 3 B only, 4 A and C only, 5 all three, 6 B and C only, 7 C only, 8 outside all three.      A three-set Venn diagram that adds up its own totals. Type a count in each region.     Placing Numbers   , is the set of odd numbers, the set of prime numbers and the set of factors of in . List the elements in each of the eight regions.    , , . Start with the centre.  Region (all three): . Region : . Region : . Region : . Region : . Region : . Region : . Region : .  Region is empty: every prime in is odd or a factor of . An empty region is still drawn.     Crops in Bungoma   An agricultural officer asked farmers in Bungoma which of maize, beans and bananas they grow. grow maize, beans and bananas; grow maize and beans, maize and bananas, and beans and bananas; grow all three. How many grow maize only? How many grow exactly two of the crops? How many grow none of them?      Centre: .  Exactly two: maize and beans only ; maize and bananas only ; beans and bananas only .  Only one: maize ; beans ; bananas .  None: the seven inner regions add up to , so none .  Check: maize , beans , bananas .    So grow maize only, grow exactly two crops and grow none.   Crops grown by farmers in Bungoma.   A three-set Venn diagram, maize, beans and bananas, with the number of farmers in each region.  Maize only ; maize and beans only ; beans only ; maize and bananas only ; all three ; beans and bananas only ; bananas only ; none .        Reading the Diagram   Use . How many farmers grow at least two of the crops? Exactly one? Beans but not bananas?    At least two: . Exactly one: . Beans but not bananas: beans only and maize and beans only, .    Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example {2, 5, 9} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Type the empty set as {} . Type a count as a whole number.                , is the set of factors of , the set of square numbers and the set of multiples of in . Place the numbers in a three-set diagram, with , , in the places of , , .  Region  ;  ;  ;  ;  ;  ;  ;     Give the region numbers of the elements (a) in only; (b) in and but not ; (c) in at least one of and .  (a) (b) (c)    In a survey of households in Kakamega, have a radio, a television and a smartphone; have a radio and a television, a radio and a smartphone, and a television and a smartphone; have all three. How many have a radio only? Exactly two of the three? None of them?  Radio only ; exactly two ; none  Centre . Exactly two: , , , total . Only: radio , television , smartphone . Inner regions , so none .    In , how many households have at least two of the three? Exactly one?  At least two ; exactly one    Explain why the centre must be filled before the exactly two regions.  Each pair total includes the people in all three sets, so the centre must be known before it can be taken away from each pair total.     "
