  "type": "Section",
  "number": "2.1",
  "title": "Proportional Parts",
  "body": " Proportional Parts       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.2 Compound Proportions, Rates of Work and Mixtures    Specific Learning Outcomes  Divide quantities using ratios in different situations           Where do you meet ratios and proportions in your home, school and community?   A family shares the harvest among those who worked on the shamba. A road contractor plans how many workers a job needs and for how long. A school fills its water tank with a pump while the kitchen draws water out. A cook mixes maize and beans for githeri and needs to know what each plate costs. Each of these is a question about ratio and proportion.  This chapter starts with sharing in a ratio, then builds up to compound proportion, where three or more quantities change at once, to rates of work, and to mixtures. Keep the key inquiry question in mind as you work.   Working in This Chapter    A ratio is written with spaces, , and its order matters: the ratio of A to B is is not the same as .  To share in a ratio, find the total number of parts, then one part, then each share as a number of parts. Check that the shares add back to the total.  Share in proportion to some amounts means share in the ratio of those amounts. In this book indirect proportion and inverse proportion mean the same thing.  We say worker-hours , worker-days and learner-hours for the size of a job.  Round only at the end. Give money to the nearest shilling unless the work is exact, times, masses and volumes exactly or to 1 decimal place, and fractions of a job as fractions in their simplest form. Numbers of workers or drivers are rounded up, and we say why.  Prices, quantities and times in this chapter are invented, at about 2026 levels.        Sharing in a Ratio   Sharing Bottle Tops   Work in groups of three. Your group has bottle tops.     Share the bottle tops in the ratio . How many does each of you get?    Share them again in the ratio .    Share them in the ratio .    Try to share them in the ratio . What goes wrong?    Is sharing equally always fair? When would sharing in a ratio be fairer?        Sharing in a Ratio  A ratio such as compares quantities. Its numbers are parts , and the amount each person gets is a share . To share a total in the ratio :  Total number of parts: .  One part: total .  Each share: its number of parts one part.  Check: the shares add up to the total.  So the first person gets of the total. Find one part first, then each share is a number of parts.    Sharing bottle tops in the ratio with a bar model.   Three bars of 5, 7 and 8 equal boxes, each box marked 3, make 20 parts. 60 bottle tops make each part 3, so the shares are 15, 21 and 24.      Stationery for Three Classes   A school shares exercise books among classes 12A, 12B and 12C in the ratio . How many does each class get?    Total parts: . One part: books.  12A gets , 12B gets and 12C gets exercise books.  Check: .     Shares from One Share   Achieng, Baraka and Chebet share the profit from their poultry project in the ratio . Chebet gets Ksh . Find the total profit and the other two shares.    Chebet's parts are Ksh , so one part is .  There are parts, so the total profit is , Ksh . Achieng gets and Baraka : Ksh and Ksh .     Shares from a Difference   Two dairy co-operatives share their income from a milk cooler in the ratio . The first gets Ksh more than the second. Find each share and the total.    The difference is parts, so parts are Ksh and one part is .  The first gets and the second : Ksh and Ksh , Ksh in all.    Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.        Share Ksh in the ratio .  Ksh and Ksh    Divide of fertiliser among three farmers in the ratio .  , and  parts, and one part is . The shares are , and .    A matatu SACCO shares Ksh among three drivers, Otieno, Kamau and Wekesa, in the ratio of the days they worked, . How much does each driver get?  Otieno Ksh ; Kamau and Wekesa Ksh each    Mangoes are sorted into grades A, B and C in the ratio . There are grade A mangoes. How many mangoes are there altogether, and how many are grade C?  mangoes; grade C  parts are , so one part is . There are parts, mangoes, and grade C is .    Money is shared in the ratio . The larger share is Ksh more than the smaller. Find the total.  Ksh  The difference is parts, so one part is . The total is .    Challenge: Which Totals Share Exactly?  Can bottle tops be shared exactly in the ratio ? Which totals from to can be?  How many parts are there? Which totals divide exactly by that number?  No; and  There are parts, and is not a multiple of . The multiples of from to are and .       Proportional Parts in Practice   Fair Shares in a Chama   Three members of a chama put Ksh , Ksh and Ksh into a posho mill. This month the mill made a profit of Ksh .     Write the ratio of their contributions in its simplest form.    Share the profit in this ratio.    How much would each get if the profit were shared equally?    Who gains and who loses by sharing equally? Which way is fairer here, and why?        Proportional Parts  To share in proportion to some amounts, share in the ratio of those amounts (simplify the ratio first if you like). Contributions of Ksh , and give the ratio .  A ratio of fractions such as becomes a ratio of whole numbers when every fraction is multiplied by the lowest common multiple of the denominators, here : .  To increase in the ratio  or decrease in the ratio  , multiply by the first number over the second: new old , or old .    A Ratio Made of Fractions   Share Ksh in the ratio .    The lowest common multiple of , and is . Multiplying each fraction by gives , which is parts.  One part is , so the shares are Ksh , Ksh and Ksh .     Increase and Decrease in a Ratio   (a) A jua kali workshop makes jiko a week. Its output increases in the ratio . How many does it make now?  (b) A bus fare of Ksh decreases in the ratio . What is the new fare?    (a) jiko a week.  (b) , so Ksh .     Sharing a Repair Bill   Three neighbours keep , and cows that drink from a shared borehole. They share a repair bill of Ksh in proportion to the number of cows. How much does each pay?    The ratio simplifies to , which is parts. One part is .  They pay Ksh , Ksh and Ksh .    Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.            Share Ksh in the ratio .  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh  Multiply by : , parts. One part is .    (a) Increase in the ratio . (b) Decrease Ksh in the ratio .  (a) (b) Ksh    Three schools paid Ksh , Ksh and Ksh for a joint trip to Hell's Gate. A refund of Ksh is shared in proportion to what they paid. How much does each school get back?  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh    The price of a phone rose in the ratio to Ksh . What was the old price?  Ksh  New old , so old .    Three casual workers worked , and days on a building site. They share Ksh in proportion to the days they worked. How much does each get?  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh    Challenge: Combining Two Ratios  and . Find , then share Ksh in this ratio.  Make the two values of the same: what is the lowest common multiple of and ?  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh  Make equal to in both: and , so . That is parts, and one part is .     "
