  "id": "sec-linear-inequalities-in-two-unknowns",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-linear-inequalities-in-two-unknowns.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "3.2",
  "title": "Linear Inequalities in Two Unknowns",
  "body": " Linear Inequalities in Two Unknowns       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.3 Linear Inequalities    Specific Learning Outcomes  Form linear inequalities in two unknowns from real life scenarios          Shopping for School   Exercise books cost Ksh each and pens cost Ksh each. Achieng has Ksh to spend.     What do books and pens cost? Can she afford them?    Write an expression for the cost of books and pens.    Write an inequality that says she can afford them.    Test the pairs , and .    She needs at least books. Write this as an inequality.    Give two more pairs that meet both conditions.    Why must and be whole numbers that are not negative?        Inequalities in Two Unknowns  An inequality such as is a linear inequality in two unknowns . The sign can be any of , , or .  A solution is a pair of values that makes the inequality true. To test a pair, substitute both values and see whether the statement is true. There are usually many solutions.  When and count things, they are whole numbers and cannot be negative, so we also write and .  We write inequalities with whole-number coefficients in their simplest form: if every number has a common factor, divide by it. Dividing both sides by a positive number does not change which pairs make the inequality true.    Mandazi and Chapati   Wanjiru sells mandazi and chapati. She makes a profit of Ksh on each mandazi and Ksh on each chapati. She wants a profit of at least Ksh a day. Write an inequality for mandazi and chapati. Does selling mandazi and chapati meet her target? Does selling mandazi and chapati?    The profit is shillings, and at least gives : .  : , and is true: she meets the target exactly.  : , and is false: she does not.     Seats for a School Trip   A school takes people on a trip. A bus has seats and a matatu has seats. Write an inequality for buses and matatus to carry everyone, and simplify it. Are buses and matatus enough? Are buses and matatus?    The seats are , and there must be at least : . Every number is a multiple of , so divide by : .  : , that is seats: exactly enough.  : , that is seats: not enough.     Three Times as Many   A juice stall sells cups of mango juice and cups of passion juice a day. It always sells at least three times as many cups of mango as of passion. Write an inequality. Do mango and passion fit? Do mango and passion?    Three times the passion cups is , and the mango cups are at least that: .  A common mistake is to write . Check with a pair that fits the words, , : is true, as it should be. Now try , , which does not fit the words: is false for , but is true for , so is wrong.  : , yes. : is false, no.    Type as <= and as >= , for example 3x+10y>=30 . Use the letters x and y as defined in the question. Type fractions, not decimals, for example y<=2x\/3+1 .            Adult tickets for a school play cost Ksh and learner tickets cost Ksh . The drama club needs takings of at least Ksh . Write an inequality for adult and learner tickets, and simplify it.  , which simplifies to .  Takings are from adults and from learners. At least gives : . Divide every term by : .    Daily data bundles cost Ksh and weekly bundles cost Ksh . Otieno spends at most Ksh a month on daily and weekly bundles. Write the inequality.     For the school play in , which of these pairs meet the target: , , ?  : Ksh , yes. : Ksh , no. : Ksh , yes.  Substitute into . : , yes. : , less than , no. : ; the target is at least , so exactly meets it, yes.    A choir has at least girls for every boys. Write an inequality for girls and boys.  (or )    A lorry driver travels for hours at on the highway and hours at through towns. The driver covers at most in a day. Write the inequality and simplify it.  , which simplifies to .  Distance is speed times time, so the distance is . Divide every number by . Keeping to the lower limit in towns protects pedestrians and other road users.    Challenge: The Same Inequality  Show that and have exactly the same solutions.  What happens when you divide by ?  Dividing both sides of the first by the positive number gives the second, so a pair makes one true exactly when it makes the other true.  For any pair, and . Dividing both sides of an inequality by a positive number keeps it true or keeps it false, so the two inequalities are true for exactly the same pairs.    "
