  "number": "3.3",
  "title": "Graphical Representation of Linear Inequalities",
  "body": " Graphical Representation of Linear Inequalities       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.3 Linear Inequalities    Specific Learning Outcomes  Represent linear inequalities in two unknowns graphically          Which Side of the Line?   Work with a partner on graph paper, with and from to .     Work out at , , , , and .    Draw the line through and .    Plot the six points. Which are on the line, which are below it and which are above it?    Shade the side where is false.    Should the line be solid or dashed for ? For ?    Why is one test point enough to decide which side to shade?        Drawing an Inequality    Draw the boundary line: replace the sign by . This is the line where the two sides are equal. Find where it crosses the axes: put to find where it crosses the -axis, then put to find where it crosses the -axis. Draw the line through these two points.  Draw the line solid for or (points on the line are included) and dashed for or (they are not). Solid means the line is included, dashed means it is not.  Choose a test point that is not on the line: , or or if the line passes through the origin. Substitute it. If the inequality is true, the test point is on the wanted side; if it is false, it is on the unwanted side. One point tells you the whole side.  Shade the side where the inequality is false. Leave the wanted side clear and label it , the required region . Add the key Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ( ), the required region.    An inequality with one letter has a vertical or horizontal boundary. For , draw the vertical line ; is on the right. For , draw the horizontal line , dashed; is below.    Shading Conventions  In Grade 11, and in some books, the region you want is shaded. In this book, as in many Kenyan textbooks, we shade the region we do not want, so that the required region stays clear. This helps when several inequalities are drawn together, and in linear programming. Either way is correct if the diagram has a key that says which.    Move the sliders and press Sign. The shaded side is not wanted; is left clear. Drag to test points.     Vertical and Horizontal Boundaries   On separate axes, show (a) ; (b) , shading the unwanted region.    (a) The boundary is the horizontal line , drawn dashed because the sign is . The points with are below it, so shade above the line, where .  (b) The boundary is the vertical line , drawn solid because the sign is . The points with are on and to the right of it, so shade to the left, where .   (a) ; (b) .   Left: a dashed horizontal line y = 3, the part above shaded, R below. Right: a solid vertical line x = -1, the part to its left shaded, R to the right. Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.        A Sloping Boundary   Show the region .    Boundary . Put : , , so it crosses the -axis at . Put : , , so it crosses the -axis at .  The sign is , so the line is solid.  Test : and is true. The origin is in , so shade the side away from the origin.   The region .   A solid line through (7, 0) and (0, 3); the side away from the origin shaded; R contains the origin. Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.        A Boundary Through the Origin   Show the region .    Boundary . When , , so the line passes through the origin. Find a second point: when , , , giving .  The sign is , so the line is dashed.  The origin is on the line, so it cannot be the test point. Test : and ; is false. So is on the unwanted side: shade the side that contains .   The region .   A dashed line through (0, 0) and (2, 7); the side containing (1, 0) shaded; R on the other side, above and to the left of the line. Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.       Choose from each list. Type where a line crosses an axis as a whole number, for example -4 .            On separate axes, draw each inequality and shade the unwanted region: (a) ; (b) .  (a) A solid vertical line , shade to the right. (b) A dashed horizontal line , shade below.    Draw the region .  A dashed line through and . Test : is false, so shade the side containing the origin.    Draw the region .  A solid line through and . Test : is false, so shade the side containing the origin.    Without drawing, decide whether is in the required region of .  Yes: , and .    Draw the region for the juice stall in , , with across and up, from to on each axis.  A solid line through and . Test : is true, so shade the other side, the side containing , above the line.    Challenge: Choosing a Test Point  Explain why cannot be the test point for , and draw the region.  Substitute into .  is on the boundary , so it is on neither side. Test : is true. Draw a dashed line through and and shade the side containing .    "
