  "body": " Translation Vector       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.2 Vectors    Specific Learning Outcomes  Draw an image of an object under a translation vector on a Cartesian plane          Sliding a Triangle   Draw the triangle with vertices , and . Slide it squares right and squares up: the move .     Draw the new triangle and label its vertices , and .    Write , and as column vectors.    Compare the side lengths of the two triangles, and the way they face.    Which move takes the new triangle back to the first one?    Where on the roads near your school have you seen one painted shape repeated by sliding it?        Triangle and its image under the translation by .   Triangle A(1, 1), B(4, 1), C(1, 3) and its image A'(6, 3), B'(9, 3), C'(6, 5), with equal arrows from each vertex to its image.      Translations  A translation slides a shape without turning it: every point of the shape, the object , moves by the same vector to give the image . That vector is the translation vector  .  Under the translation by the vector , the point moves to . Image points are labelled with a dash: is read P dash . The translation vector is image minus object, , and the translation by takes the image back to the object.  The image has the same size and shape as the object and faces the same way.    Drag the vertices of the triangle and move the and sliders. Every vertex moves by the same vector .     Image of a Shape   The quadrilateral has vertices , , and . Find its image under the translation by the vector .    Add to each -coordinate and to each -coordinate:  , , , .     Finding the Translation Vector   A translation maps to . Find the translation vector, check it with and , and write the translation that takes the image back.    Image minus object: .  Check: moves to , which is .  The translation by takes the image back.     Three arrows painted along a lane, seen from above.   A lane drawn from above with three identical painted arrows; the tips are at (3, 4), (3, 19) and (3, 34), each 15 m beyond the last.      Arrows Along a Lane   A road crew paints arrows along a lane ( ). On their plan, in metres, the tip of the first arrow is at , and each next arrow is the previous one translated by . Find the tips of the second and third arrows, and the single translation that takes the first arrow to the third.    Second tip: . Third tip: .  The first arrow goes to the third by .  Lane arrows tell drivers and riders which way each lane goes. Keeping to them prevents head-on crashes, so they are painted again and again along the road, each one a translation of the last.    Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example -3 . Drag each point on the grid; it jumps to the nearest grid point. Every point must be moved before you check.               Vectors Around Us  We began the chapter by asking where vectors are used in everyday life. Here are some answers.    Road arrows and direction signs give every road user a direction as well as a distance. The arrows along a lane and the stripes of a zebra crossing are translations of one shape.  Engineers add the forces on a handcart, a bridge or a building to find the resultant force each part must carry.  A weather forecast gives the wind's speed and direction: a velocity. Rain clouds are moved by the wind.  A phone's map gives directions such as in turn right , and works out straight-line distances from coordinates.  Computer games move a character on the screen by adding vectors many times a second.    Where else in your community could you use a vector to describe a move, a push or a direction?     Find the image of under the translation by , and of under the translation by .  ;    Translate the triangle , , by . Check that the side lengths are unchanged.  , , ; the sides are , and in both triangles.    A translation maps to . Find the translation vector.     Under the translation by , the image of is . Find .     On a road plan in metres, the tip of a lane arrow is at . Each next arrow is the previous one translated by . Where is the tip of the fourth arrow?     Challenge: Two Translations  Translate by to , then translate by to . Which single translation takes straight to ?  Add the two vectors.  , ; the translation by .  and . The single translation is the sum ; check: .    "
