  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "7.1.1.5",
  "title": "Challenge: Where Great Circles Meet.",
  "body": " Challenge: Where Great Circles Meet  Explain why any two different great circles on a sphere cross at exactly two points, and why those two points are at opposite ends of a diameter.  Both planes pass through the centre.  The two planes meet in a line through the centre, which meets the sphere at the two ends of a diameter.  The planes of the two great circles are different, and both contain the centre . Two different planes that share a point meet in a straight line, so they meet in a line through . The circles cross where this line meets the sphere: at two points, each a distance from on opposite sides of it, that is, at the two ends of a diameter.  "
