  "type": "Section",
  "number": "8.5",
  "title": "Hire Purchase",
  "body": " Hire Purchase       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.4 Commercial Arithmetic III    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine hire purchase costs of goods          The Fridge Advert   A shop in Kisumu advertises a fridge: Cash price Ksh . Or take it home today for a deposit of Ksh and monthly instalments of Ksh .     Find the total of the instalments.    Find the total paid on hire purchase.    How much more than the cash price is this?    Write the extra as a percentage of the cash price.    Who owns the fridge after the sixth instalment has been paid?        Hire Purchase  The cash price is what you pay if you pay everything at once. Under hire purchase you pay a deposit now, take the goods home, and pay the rest in equal instalments , usually monthly. Then and the extra paid is the hire purchase price minus the cash price. With hire purchase you use the goods now and pay more than the cash price.    The fridge of on hire purchase: a deposit today and twelve monthly instalments, compared with the cash price.   A time line from today to month 12. A tall bar today is the deposit of 7000 shillings; twelve shorter bars, one each month, are instalments of 3100 shillings. On the right the deposit and the instalments are stacked to 44 200 shillings, beside a bar for the cash price of 38 500 shillings; a double arrow marks the extra 5700 shillings.  Horizontal axis: months from today, 0 to 12. At month 0 a bar of height 7000 labelled deposit 7000. At each month from 1 to 12 a bar of height 3100; one label over them reads 12 instalments of 3100. To the right, two columns: a stacked column of 7000 plus twelve blocks of 3100 reaching 44 200, labelled hire purchase 44 200, and a column of height 38 500 labelled cash 38 500. A dashed line at 38 500 runs across to the stacked column, and a double arrow between 38 500 and 44 200 is labelled extra 5700.      Who Owns It?  Under hire purchase the goods belong to the seller until the final instalment is paid. If you miss payments, the seller can take the goods back, and what you have already paid may be lost. Before you sign, check the hire purchase price, the number of instalments and what happens if you cannot pay.    A Motorbike on Hire Purchase   A motorbike costs Ksh cash. On hire purchase the deposit is of the cash price, followed by monthly instalments of Ksh . Find the hire purchase price and the extra paid.    Deposit: of is . Instalments: . The hire purchase price is Ksh and the extra paid is , Ksh .     Finding the Instalment   A smartphone costs Ksh cash. Its hire purchase price is Ksh : a deposit of Ksh and then equal monthly instalments. Find each instalment.    The instalments pay the hire purchase price after the deposit: . Each instalment is , Ksh .     Cash, Hire Purchase or a SACCO Loan?   For the motorbike in , a third choice is to borrow the cash price of Ksh from a SACCO at per annum simple interest and repay it all over months. Which choice costs least?    months is years. The SACCO is repaid Hire purchase costs Ksh , Ksh less than the SACCO loan here. Paying cash, Ksh , is cheapest of all, if you have the money.    Type amounts as plain numbers, without Ksh, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give money to the nearest shilling and percentages to 1 decimal place unless the question says otherwise; exact answers in cents are also accepted.                A television costs Ksh cash, or Ksh deposit and monthly instalments of Ksh . Find the hire purchase price and the extra paid.  Hire purchase price Ksh ; extra Ksh    A water tank costs Ksh cash. On hire purchase the deposit is of the cash price, followed by instalments of Ksh . Find the deposit, the hire purchase price and the extra paid.  Deposit Ksh ; hire purchase price Ksh ; extra Ksh    A cooker's hire purchase price is Ksh : a deposit of Ksh and equal monthly instalments. Find each instalment.  Ksh    A solar kit costs Ksh cash. Offer A: Ksh deposit and monthly instalments of Ksh . Offer B: Ksh deposit and monthly instalments of Ksh . Which offer costs less, and how much more than the cash price does each cost?  A costs Ksh and B Ksh , so A costs Ksh less; extras Ksh and Ksh  A: , extra . B: , extra . A is cheaper by , but B asks for a smaller deposit and smaller monthly payments.    A bicycle shop's hire purchase price for a bicycle is Ksh , with monthly instalments of Ksh . What deposit is paid?  Ksh  The instalments total , so the deposit is .    Challenge: Is the Extra Worth It?  A sewing machine costs Ksh cash, or Ksh deposit and monthly instalments of Ksh . Find the extra as a percentage of the cash price, and give one good reason for choosing hire purchase anyway.  Find the hire purchase price first.  Extra Ksh , of the cash price. A tailor can earn money with the machine from the first month instead of saving for a year first.  Hire purchase price ; extra ; . A good reason: the machine earns money from the start, which can pay the instalments.    "
