  "number": "10.3",
  "title": "Laws of Probability",
  "body": " Laws of Probability       Strand  3.0 Statistics and Probability    Sub-Strand  3.2 Probability III    Specific Learning Outcomes  Apply laws of probability to predict likelihood of events          Beads in Two Bags   Bag 1 holds red, blue and yellow beads. Bag 2 holds white beads and black bead.     One bead is taken from bag 1. Find , , and by counting. Compare the last one with .    Why can one bead not be red and yellow at the same time? Why does that make adding work?    Now take one bead from each bag. How many different pairs are possible? How many pairs are red and white? Compare with .    Does the bead you take from bag 1 change what can happen with bag 2?        Laws of Probability  Two events are mutually exclusive if they cannot both happen at the same time, like a bead being red and being yellow. Two events are independent if one happening does not change the probability of the other, like the beads from two separate bags.    Complement: , where is read not .  Addition law, for mutually exclusive events and : . The different outcomes of one experiment are always mutually exclusive.  Multiplication law, for independent events and : .     and is the intersection and or is the union , as in ; in this chapter we write the words. or always includes the case where both happen.    Add or Multiply?  If adding two probabilities gives more than 1, you should not have added: the events were not mutually exclusive, and perhaps the question asked for and . If multiplying gives a tiny probability for something that is likely, check whether the question asked for or .    Getting to School   Each learner at a school in Machakos travels to school in one way only. For a learner chosen at random, , and ; the rest cycle. Find (a) , (b) , (c) .    The four ways are mutually exclusive and one of them must happen, so their probabilities add to 1.  (a) .  (b) Addition law: .  (c) Complement: .     A Coin and a Die   A coin is tossed and a die is rolled. Find the probability of a head and a number greater than 4.    The coin and the die are independent. and , so Check with the list of outcomes in : H5 and H6 give .     Passion-Fruit Seedlings   A farmer buys one passion-fruit seedling from nursery A and one from nursery B. A seedling from A survives with probability and one from B with probability , independently. Find the probability that (a) both survive, (b) A's seedling survives and B's does not.    (a) .  (b) , so the probability is .     The Same Score Twice   A die is rolled twice. Find the probability of (a) two sixes, (b) the same score both times.    (a) The rolls are independent: .  (b) The same score happens in six mutually exclusive ways: two 1s, two 2s, ..., two 6s. Each has probability by the multiplication law, so by the addition law the probability is . Check: the six doubles lie on the diagonal of the -cell grid.     Probability in daily life: a tea factory manager uses the multiplication law to estimate the chance that two machines that work independently both break down on the same day, and so how many spare parts to keep. Where else might two independent risks be combined?  Type a fraction with a slash, for example 5\/12 , and a decimal with a point, for example 0.35 . Give probabilities exactly: do not round.            At a juice kiosk in Nakuru, each customer orders one juice. A customer orders mango juice with probability and passion juice with probability ; every other customer orders avocado juice. Find (a) , (b) .  (a) (b)    The probability that Otieno's alarm fails is and the probability that his matatu is late is , independently. Find the probability that (a) both happen, (b) neither happens.  (a) (b)  (a) . (b) .    A coin is tossed and a die is rolled. Find the probability of (a) a tail and an odd number, (b) a head and a 6. Check your answers by listing the outcomes.  (a) (b)    Two dice are rolled. Find the probability that (a) both show an even number, (b) the first shows 1 and the second shows 6.  (a) (b)    and are independent, and . Find .   , so .    Challenge: A Wet Weekend?  Kioko says: The probability of rain on Saturday is and on Sunday is , so the probability of rain at the weekend is . What is wrong? Assuming the two days are independent, find the probability of rain on at least one of the two days.  First find the probability of no rain on either day.  The events are not mutually exclusive (it can rain on both days), and no probability can be . The probability is .  P(no rain on either day) , so P(rain on at least one day) .    "
